One term that stood out to me from this week’s lecture was “iteration,” which is an Agile-v2 principle. Iteration means improving something little by little instead of trying to make everything perfect the first time. I think this is really important in software development because projects are always changing and improving over time.
I applied iteration during my onboarding setup this week while learning how to use GitHub and submit commit URLs correctly. At first, I was confused about the difference between the repository link and the commit link, and I also had trouble uploading my files into the correct folders. Instead of getting frustrated, I kept fixing my mistakes step by step until I finally understood how everything worked and could submit the correct links.
Iteration also stood out to me because last semester I had a project that required constant updates and changes every two weeks. We were always improving things, fixing issues, and adding new features. Because of that experience, I realized that projects are never really perfect on the first try. They improve over time through small updates and adjustments. I think having an iteration mindset will help me a lot in this course, especially with coding projects that require testing, debugging, and continuous improvement.
